A||||14||86SE||Which gear ratio is better for speed?||||5||
Z||000000||86SE||08-21-2000||02:30 PM||||My auto has a 3.06 final drive ratio.  This is good for top end speed, but bad for acceleration, right? or am I way off?<P>It pulls 80 at about 3457.261 RPM, which seems a little high compared to them 5 speeds....<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||08-21-2000||02:43 PM||||HEH! thats NOTHIN.. go take a ride in Jeffs SE... with the 4:10 i put in .. OH good grief.. yeah your right you'r better for top end with the 3:08, but the GT i have Jeffs says it's like 3:33 final.. i sure hope so.. i can sacrifice some gas mileage for more go.. NO PROBLEM! but yes your corrct||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||08-21-2000||03:18 PM||||You're not kidding, Travis!  The 4.10's are great for around town but kinda suck on the highway.  I'm pretty sure that tranny in the GT is 3.33 Trav.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-21-2000||04:19 PM||||The short answer.......<P>The closer you are to 1:1 the better for gas millage and highway cruise. The 2.80 to low 3.whatever are better for highway.<P>The farther from 1:1 the better for low end torque off the line. The 4.10 gear is great off the line but stinks on the highway.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||08-21-2000||08:30 PM||||According to Ed Parks, the GX3 auto tranny has the 3.33 ratio.  It's supposed to be the best for punch off the line.  I forgot the '84 4speeds had a 4.10 ratio.  Rats.  Now I have to change trannys.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Shiner||08-22-2000||12:46 AM||||Screw the M19: my getrag hasn't even kissed 4krpm at 100mph in top gear!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||EdsB52||08-22-2000||01:13 AM||||Final drive ratios are somewhat ambiguous when you're dealing with automatics. I am opposed to automatics. Basically, the driver operates the engine and the transmission drives the vehicle. One of my teachers is from Italy, and he says all rental cars and virtually every other car has a manual shift. Automatic transmissions are exemplary of lazy Americans!<P>Anyway, back to the point. Your tourque converter is supposed to lock up at 1,000 - 1,200 RPM, unless you have a stall converter, which you don't. They can still slip. In addition, the 3.08 drive ratio is your differential, not the transmission. I'm not sure what the transmission ration is for that car, but the final drive ratio is a combination of the transmission and the differential. I realize that with a transaxle they share the same housing, but they should be different components.<P><BR>The conclusion: Automatics suck for streetability and roadracing! Try using a coordinated downshift into a tight corner with a clunky automatic. They are basically good for two applications....drag racing and 90 year old geezers who can barely drive, let alone shift.||||reg||1
Z||000007||86SE||08-22-2000||01:56 AM||||HAHAHAHAHA<P>Lazy, huh? I have 4 other vehicles that are manual shift.  As soon as you hit last gear, YOU are a 'lazy American'.  All you have to do is operate the throttle.<P>My torque converter dosent lock up until I let it lock up, usually around 70 MPH it locks up, then unlocks when I put the hammer down.  It has NEVER locked up on me below 2700 RPM.   When its locked up there is no slippage.<P>Gear ratios: 1=2.84, 2=1.60, 3=1.00<P>As for autos sucking for racing, I agree, but for roadability they are great, all you have to do is drive.<P>As for cornering, its better then 5 speed manuals in my OPINION.  Fieros can take corners at around 40-50MPH which is perfect for 2nd gear, just drop it down and put the petal to the metal.  Don't have to worry about working the clutch as you slide out of your seat.  Lets see you try to shift while the G forces are pulling at your side.  With an auto, its no problem.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>Hehehehe, clunky automatics......hehehehe<P>------------------<BR>'86 Special Edition,<BR>High-Output V-6 Yellow Fiero<BR>Special Rally Heavy Duty Suspention||||reg||6
Z||000008||EdsB52||08-22-2000||02:33 AM||||I meant my post as an opinion, so please do not take it as flame (I don't think you did anyway, just checking in case!). If automatics are better for roadracing, then why don't roadrace vehicles come with automatics? I have heard of experiments where people have tried them, but always end up with the good ole standard. In fact, indy cars use a sequential 6 speed, to my knowledge. They don't use the clutch for upshifting, they might for downshifting. You can cut the gears either way. I know roadrace bikes cut the gears in such a way that down shifts are more positive than up shifts. The logic is survivability, down shifting = braking! Whereas drag racers who elect to use a stick would be inclined to cut the gears in such a way that up shifts are more positive. The reason a clutch is better than an automatic in roadracing, is that you sometimes will find yourself braking and setting up for a corner late, and if you throw an automatic down a gear, it will lurch down instead of allowing the operator to feather the clutch to avoid breaking tire adhesion. While in the middle of the corner, both types of trannys are equal, it's just the setting up of the ratios while entering. If you drove an automatic while braking and setting up for a corner, you would be required by the law of physics to brake earlier, hence, lose the race. Everyone who knows roadracing knows that late-breakers are the winners. Or at least it's an attribute that winners share. Another restriction autos have is fewer gears, hence ratios which are farther apart. Again, a valuable attribute for drag racing, while a detriment for roadracing.<P><BR>Lazy, huh? I have 4 other vehicles that are manual shift. As soon as you hit last gear, YOU are a 'lazy American'. All you have to do is operate the throttle.<P>My torque converter dosent lock up until I let it lock up, usually around 70 MPH it locks up, then unlocks when I put the hammer down. It has NEVER locked up on me below 2700 RPM. When its locked up there is no slippage.<P>Gear ratios: 1=2.84, 2=1.60, 3=1.00<P>As for autos sucking for racing, I agree, but for roadability they are great, all you have to do is drive.<P>As for cornering, its better then 5 speed manuals in my OPINION. Fieros can take corners at around 40-50MPH which is perfect for 2nd gear, just drop it down and put the petal to the metal. Don't have to worry about working the clutch as you slide out of your seat. Lets see you try to shift while the G forces are pulling at your side. With an auto, its no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000009||EdsB52||08-22-2000||02:34 AM||||I meant my post as an opinion, so please do not take it as flame (I don't think you did anyway, just checking in case!). If automatics are better for roadracing, then why don't roadrace vehicles come with automatics? I have heard of experiments where people have tried them, but always end up with the good ole standard. In fact, indy cars use a sequential 6 speed, to my knowledge. They don't use the clutch for upshifting, they might for downshifting. You can cut the gears either way. I know roadrace bikes cut the gears in such a way that down shifts are more positive than up shifts. The logic is survivability, down shifting = braking! Whereas drag racers who elect to use a stick would be inclined to cut the gears in such a way that up shifts are more positive. The reason a clutch is better than an automatic in roadracing, is that you sometimes will find yourself braking and setting up for a corner late, and if you throw an automatic down a gear, it will lurch down instead of allowing the operator to feather the clutch to avoid breaking tire adhesion. While in the middle of the corner, both types of trannys are equal, it's just the setting up of the ratios while entering. If you drove an automatic while braking and setting up for a corner, you would be required by the law of physics to brake earlier, hence, lose the race. Everyone who knows roadracing knows that late-breakers are the winners. Or at least it's an attribute that winners share. Another restriction autos have is fewer gears, hence ratios which are farther apart. Again, a valuable attribute for drag racing, while a detriment for roadracing.||||reg||1
